<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kham Fu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Phaya Kham Fu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> Fu was a king of Lan Na in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> in the 1330s, a descendant of the founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a>. Northern Thai chronicles credit him with annexing the neighbouring kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> around 1338, adding its lands to Lan Na. His son and successor Pha Yu later re-established the court at Chiang Mai.</p></div>
